LUNA SCOUT: Lunar South Pole Mission Planning & Horizon Analysis System
1. Problem Statement & Background

The lunar south pole is the focal point of NASA’s Artemis campaign and future lunar base architecture due to permanently shadowed craters containing water-ice deposits. However, surface survival depends entirely on two critical, competing constraints:

Solar Power: The Sun circles near the horizon (within ~1.5°), making local topographical ridges and crater rims decisive in whether a lander or rover receives sunlight or freezes in shadow.
Direct-to-Earth (DTE) Telemetry: Earth wobbles low along the horizon over a 27.3-day libration cycle. When Earth sinks behind crater elevations, surface assets lose direct line-of-sight communication.

Legacy ephemeris and trajectory planning tools are built for dedicated astrodynamics specialists, requiring high barrier-to-entry command-line or monolithic software interfaces that make rapid cross-site evaluation tedious and inefficient.

2. Our Solution

LUNA SCOUT bridges scientific precision and high-performance user experience. It delivers a fast, client-side web application capable of:

Interactive 3D Orbital Exploration: Rotating and inspecting the Moon with candidate Artemis landing zones clearly mapped with real lunar coordinates and procedural elevation textures.
First-Person Ground / Surface Horizon View: A smooth camera dive from orbit directly onto the landing site, showing where the Sun and Earth appear relative to realistic horizon masks and azimuth/elevation polar plots.
Multi-Site Comparative Decision Engine: Rapid side-by-side assessment across 6 candidate regions (Shackleton Rim, Malapert Massif, Connecting Ridge, Peak Near Shackleton, Haworth Plains, and Nobile Rim) with custom weighted mission feasibility scoring.
Power & Telemetry Timeline Heatmaps: High-density timeline matrices revealing combined power-comms availability, solar-only survival windows, and critical blackout periods.
3. NASA Data Sources & Scientific Pipeline

Our calculations and modeling rely strictly on authentic NASA datasets:

JPL NAIF SPICE Planetary Ephemerides (de440.bsp): High-precision positions of the Sun and Earth.
Lunar Frame & Orientation Kernels (moon_pa_de440, moon_080317.tf): Coordinate transformations into Moon-fixed Principal Axis (PA) and Mean Earth (ME) frames.
NASA PDS Geosciences LOLA DEMs: Topographical elevation profiles used to model realistic local horizon masks (determining true line-of-sight elevation cutoffs rather than assuming a flat sphere).
Validation: Cross-verified against published JPL Horizons lunar surface observer output.
4. Key Standout Features
Zero-Friction Architecture: Runs instantly in any modern web browser without heavy installations, node runtimes, or external API dependencies during demonstration.
Minimalist Aesthetic: Styled with warm gold and amber glassmorphism, high-contrast dark space backgrounds, and clear telemetry HUDs designed for mission control readability.
Mission Scoring Model: Generates an objective, composite score :
Score=(Solar %×0.40)+(Comms %×0.35)+(Overlap %×0.25)
Mission Documentation Export: Single-click CSV export formatting solar and RF ephemeris telemetry for external engineering tools.
